{"authored" [{"question" "q-20070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/fa1c1c3ae2769f45854de4f0b00fc394fccb40b40704cfd09e6eb11eee3c07af.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=3{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "f26009d6e94d07cbf154d89130c1d7d423b01243d5d77ac4c27f2b79e765a456" "basis" 2233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-20070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{e}^{2\\theta }+9{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{18{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "f26009d6e94d07cbf154d89130c1d7